    "reasoning": "All 16 fields agree mathematically with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-134043: the plotted point at 7 hours is (7,85), giving 85%. q-111582: take LCM(2,7)=14 and retain the maximum binomial exponent 2. q-76457: take LCM(10,15)=30 and the maximum exponent 6. q-76475: take LCM(20,10,12)=60 and maximum exponents 7 for x and 3 for y+2. q-103836: cotangent has asymptotes at integer multiples of π, domain excluding those multiples, and period π, so only III is true. q-70669: cotangent is unbounded below, is undefined at -4π, and vanishes at -3π/2, so I and III are true. q-103840: shifting by -9π leaves cotangent unchanged. q-66675: periodicity gives √3·√3−4=-1. q-158055: cotangent is odd, giving -5/2. q-158064: periodicity and oddness give cot(-x+15π)=6, hence 6/2+7=10. q-36011: each separate linear branch is injective on intervals I and II; interval III contains repeated output values. q-66679: the parabola is strictly monotone on each side of its vertex x=1, making I and II invertible; III contains both sides and repeated outputs. q-87606: the population variance used by the captured solution is 117/9−(27/9)²=4. q-42657: in grid units p=(-2,0), q=(0,3), and r=(6,-6), so r=-3p-2q. q-77451: a=(-3,0), b=(-1,-2), and c=(6,-6), so c=-3a+3b. q-317285: the correct graph decreases, stays below its horizontal asymptote y=-4, and passes through (0,-5). Current option 1 matches the worked-solution graph; the source image additionally highlights the intercept. No prior submission or grade is supplied; confirmation rests on the captured worked solutions.",
